WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.000
This is Andaith, a personal scheduler I built with Claude Code’s help and use myself.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.880
It runs on my home server and connects scheduled blocks with Telegram reminders and check-ins.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:23.500
I wanted to bring my plans and my check-ins into one place. But using it taught me something.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:31.000
Asking for input every time adds friction. Sometimes, a simple reminder is enough.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:39.460
Let me show you with a fictional example. Alex is working on a project called Atlas.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:48.940
First, Alex schedules a block to prepare an example, with a start time and a duration.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:01:01.330
When the block starts, Andaith sends a reminder. Alex replies with an intention: prepare a minimal example.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:10.560
At the end, Alex records the result. The original plan stays separate from what Alex reports doing.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:19.590
If Alex doesn’t check in, Andaith records that no response was received.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:27.480
That doesn’t mean the activity didn’t happen. It just means there’s no check-in to show.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.480
External agents can also prepare digests and send them through Andaith.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:43.940
This fictional event is one example. The agent gathers the information, and Andaith handles the notification.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.020
I’m still using Andaith and exploring integrations that make sense for it.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:58.680
If you’d like to try it, or have a similar challenge, send me an email. I’d love to hear what you have in mind.
